A||||2||RFMike||Dunlop evaluation||||1||
Z||000000||RFMike||02-05-2000||12:17 AM||||In an earlier post I said I would evaluate a set of Dunlop DA 60's for winter driving.  Unfortunately, (or fortunately, depending on your viewpoint), there hasn't been a lot of snow in western Wisconsin this year.  There has been enough at this point for an opinion.<BR>These tires were rated the best buy in performance all season tires buy Consumer's Guide.  I think it was deserved.  Dry road adhesion is excellent; you would be hardpressed to break these loose in aggressive cornering.  There is very little sidewall sway.  Ride is fairly stiff, but not unreasonably so.  Cornering response is very quick.  They really are a high performance tire.<BR>Traction in snow and ice is very good.  Don't get me wrong- your Fiero will not suddenly become a 4x4.  In fact, a new set of Blizzacks will be better in both snow and ice.  These are not that far behind, and they can be run all year.  What's best is the cost.  They run about $70 for 215/60-15 and $65 for 205/60-15, which is what I had installed.  That's a great price for a tire that does what these do.<BR>I had a set of these on a front wheel drive car, and I got about 45,000 out of them.  I expect less from these, because I can't rotate them form front to back.||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||02-05-2000||01:09 PM||||I put those tires on an 83 Impala and they made a huge difference in the way the car handled. The Consumer Reports article is what made me try them. Those tires really are "best buys".||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||02-07-2000||01:20 PM||||Got a set on my 300ZX.  Better than the old Nitto 450s.  Have a pair on my Volvo 740T.  Will add a second pair to the rear when due.  I will be putting a set on the Fiero when due.  Great tire.  Awesome price.||||reg||14
